  {
    "id": "paper:867752822639165809::conclusion_2",
    "global_id": "gcn_2884f4217b4f4f9f",
    "order": 2,
    "title": "Instability source and remedy: model fragility under small weight perturbations; model averaging stabilizes out-of-distribution metrics",
    "content": "The instability of out-of-distribution (OOD) metrics near the final checkpoint arises from the trained solution being fragile to small perturbations of network weights: evaluating the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) and the false positive rate at 95% true positive rate (FPR95) as the weights are moved along random normalized directions \\(d\\) with small scalar magnitude \\(\\alpha\\) (i.e., \\(\\theta(d,\\alpha)=\\theta_{\\text{final}}+\\alpha\\cdot d\\)) shows narrow optima in the weight space causing sharp metric changes for small \\(\\alpha\\). Applying exponential moving-weight averaging (Model Averaging, MA) stabilizes OOD performance by producing a smoother / wider optimum: MA is implemented as the exponential moving average of parameters after an epoch \\(t_0\\),\n      \\[\n      \\theta_{t+1}^{\\mathrm{MA}}=\\begin{cases}\n      \\theta_t, & t<t_0,\\\\[4pt]\n      \\tau\\cdot \\theta_t^{\\mathrm{MA}} + (1-\\tau)\\cdot \\theta_{t+1}, & t\\ge t_0,\n      \\end{cases}\n      \\]\n      with \\(\\tau=\\dfrac{t-t_0}{t-t_0+1}\\). The averaged parameters \\(\\theta^{\\mathrm{MA}}\\) yield substantially reduced AUROC and FPR95 sensitivity to small weight perturbations and smoother AUROC curves during training across scoring functions.",
    "labeled_segments": {
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "The instability of out-of-distribution (OOD) metrics near the final checkpoint arises from the trained solution being fragile to small perturbations of network weights:",
          "label": "assertion [1]"
        },
        {
          "text": " evaluating the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) and the false positive rate at 95% true positive rate (FPR95) as the weights are moved along random normalized directions \\(d\\) with small scalar magnitude \\(\\alpha\\) (i.e., \\(\\theta(d,\\alpha)=\\theta_{\\text{final}}+\\alpha\\cdot d\\))",
          "label": "elaboration"
        },
        {
          "text": " shows narrow optima in the weight space causing sharp metric changes for small \\(\\alpha\\).",
          "label": "proof of the assertion [1]"
        },
        {
          "text": " Applying exponential moving-weight averaging (Model Averaging, MA) stabilizes OOD performance by producing a smoother / wider optimum:",
          "label": "assertion [2]"
        },
        {
          "text": " MA is implemented as the exponential moving average of parameters after an epoch \\(t_0\\),\n      \\[\n      \\theta_{t+1}^{\\mathrm{MA}}=\\begin{cases}\n      \\theta_t, & t<t_0,\\\\[4pt]\n      \\tau\\cdot \\theta_t^{\\mathrm{MA}} + (1-\\tau)\\cdot \\theta_{t+1}, & t\\ge t_0,\n      \\end{cases}\n      \\]\n      with \\(\\tau=\\dfrac{t-t_0}{t-t_0+1}\\).",
          "label": "elaboration"
        },
        {
          "text": " The averaged parameters \\(\\theta^{\\mathrm{MA}}\\) yield substantially reduced AUROC and FPR95 sensitivity to small weight perturbations and smoother AUROC curves during training across scoring functions.",
          "label": "proof of the assertion [2]"
        }
      ]
    },
    "labeling_status": "ok"
  },
