  {
    "id": "paper:867752822639165809::conclusion_4",
    "global_id": "gcn_c26479c7bc77476e",
    "order": 4,
    "title": "Algorithmic contribution: Average of Pruning (AoP) — combine iterative magnitude pruning with model averaging as a pluggable out-of-distribution module",
    "content": "Average of Pruning (AoP) is a practical, architecture-agnostic pipeline combining iterative magnitude pruning with parameter averaging to mitigate overfitting and instability for out-of-distribution (OOD) detection. Operationally, AoP comprises three steps: (1) apply iterative magnitude pruning (IMP) with weight-rewinding (the Lottery Ticket Hypothesis with rewind protocol): initialize mask \\(m = 1^n\\) (all weights retained), train from \\(\\theta_0\\) to \\(\\theta_k\\), then iteratively train \\(m \\odot \\theta_k\\) to \\(m \\odot \\theta_T\\), prune the lowest-magnitude 20% of weights globally to update \\(m\\), and rewind weights to \\(\\theta_k\\), repeating for a number of iterations \\(N\\) (with 20% pruning per iteration and 9 iterations reaching approximately 86% sparsity in CIFAR experiments); (2) during or after pruning, compute an exponential moving average of parameters per\n      \\[\n      \\theta_{t+1}^{\\mathrm{MA}}=\\begin{cases}\n      \\theta_t, & t<t_0,\\\\[4pt]\n      \\tau\\cdot \\theta_t^{\\mathrm{MA}} + (1-\\tau)\\cdot \\theta_{t+1}, & t\\ge t_0,\n      \\end{cases}\n      \\]\n      with \\(t_0=100\\) epochs for CIFAR training and \\(\\tau = (t-t_0)/(t-t_0+1)\\); (3) use the averaged, pruned model \\(f(x; m\\odot \\theta^{\\mathrm{MA}})\\) as a drop-in replacement for any post-hoc OOD scoring method (e.g., Maximum Softmax Probability (MSP), Out-of-DIstribution detector for Neural networks (ODIN), energy-based scoring (Energy), Virtual-Logit Matching (ViM), K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)). AoP is designed to be low-barrier: pruning reduces learned common features (addressing overfitting), and model averaging widens optima (addressing instability); the combined module is applied without modifying existing scoring functions.",
    "labeled_segments": {
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "Average of Pruning (AoP) is a practical, architecture-agnostic pipeline combining iterative magnitude pruning with parameter averaging to mitigate overfitting and instability for out-of-distribution (OOD) detection.",
          "label": "assertion [1]"
        },
        {
          "text": "Operationally, AoP comprises three steps: (1) apply iterative magnitude pruning (IMP) with weight-rewinding (the Lottery Ticket Hypothesis with rewind protocol): initialize mask \\(m = 1^n\\) (all weights retained), train from \\(\\theta_0\\) to \\(\\theta_k\\), then iteratively train \\(m \\odot \\theta_k\\) to \\(m \\odot \\theta_T\\), prune the lowest-magnitude 20% of weights globally to update \\(m\\), and rewind weights to \\(\\theta_k\\), repeating for a number of iterations \\(N\\) (with 20% pruning per iteration and 9 iterations reaching approximately 86% sparsity in CIFAR experiments); (2) during or after pruning, compute an exponential moving average of parameters per\n      \\[\n      \\theta_{t+1}^{\\mathrm{MA}}=\\begin{cases}\n      \\theta_t, & t<t_0,\\\\[4pt]\n      \\tau\\cdot \\theta_t^{\\mathrm{MA}} + (1-\\tau)\\cdot \\theta_{t+1}, & t\\ge t_0,\n      \\end{cases}\n      \\]\n      with \\(t_0=100\\) epochs for CIFAR training and \\(\\tau = (t-t_0)/(t-t_0+1)\\); (3) use the averaged, pruned model \\(f(x; m\\odot \\theta^{\\mathrm{MA}})\\) as a drop-in replacement for any post-hoc OOD scoring method (e.g., Maximum Softmax Probability (MSP), Out-of-DIstribution detector for Neural networks (ODIN), energy-based scoring (Energy), Virtual-Logit Matching (ViM), K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)).",
          "label": "elaboration"
        },
        {
          "text": "AoP is designed to be low-barrier:",
          "label": "motivation"
        },
        {
          "text": "pruning reduces learned common features (addressing overfitting),",
          "label": "assertion [2]"
        },
        {
          "text": "and model averaging widens optima (addressing instability);",
          "label": "assertion [3]"
        },
        {
          "text": "the combined module is applied without modifying existing scoring functions.",
          "label": "elaboration"
        }
      ]
    },
    "labeling_status": "ok"
  },
  {
    "id": "paper:867752822639165809::conclusion_5",
    "global_id": "gcn_9aec3c7bf65845af",
    "order": 5,
    "title": "Empirical efficacy: Average of Pruning consistently improves out-of-distribution detection metrics across datasets, scoring functions and architectures",
    "content": "Applying Average of Pruning (AoP) as a plug-in improves out-of-distribution (OOD) detection performance across multiple benchmarks and scoring methods. Representative results: on ResNet-18 trained on CIFAR-10, applying AoP to K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) increases average area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) across six OOD datasets from 94.39% to 95.04% and reduces average false positive rate at 95% true positive rate (FPR95) from 34.42% to 25.30% (a 9.1 percentage-point decrease); applying AoP to Virtual-Logit Matching (ViM) on the same backbone/dataset increases average AUROC from 94.18% to 95.69% and reduces FPR95 from 32.27% to 25.3%. AoP also improves multiple scoring functions (e.g., Maximum Softmax Probability (MSP), MaxLogit) and scales to larger datasets: on Tiny-ImageNet, AoP improves MSP AUROC by 1.5–3.3 percentage points on Textures, LSUN, and iSUN; on ImageNet, AoP yields AUROC improvements of 0.3–0.7 percentage points across scoring functions on Places, SUN, and Textures. The improvements hold across backbones (ResNet variants, WideResNet, VGG, MobileNet) and remain when combining AoP with Outlier Exposure (OE).",
    "labeled_segments": {
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "Applying Average of Pruning (AoP) as a plug-in improves out-of-distribution (OOD) detection performance across multiple benchmarks and scoring methods.",
          "label": "assertion [1]"
        },
        {
          "text": "Representative results: ",
          "label": "other"
        },
        {
          "text": "on ResNet-18 trained on CIFAR-10, applying AoP to K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) increases average area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) across six OOD datasets from 94.39% to 95.04% and reduces average false positive rate at 95% true positive rate (FPR95) from 34.42% to 25.30% (a 9.1 percentage-point decrease); applying AoP to Virtual-Logit Matching (ViM) on the same backbone/dataset increases average AUROC from 94.18% to 95.69% and reduces FPR95 from 32.27% to 25.3%.",
          "label": "example of the assertion [1]"
        },
        {
          "text": "AoP also improves multiple scoring functions (e.g., Maximum Softmax Probability (MSP), MaxLogit) and scales to larger datasets:",
          "label": "assertion [2]"
        },
        {
          "text": "on Tiny-ImageNet, AoP improves MSP AUROC by 1.5–3.3 percentage points on Textures, LSUN, and iSUN; on ImageNet, AoP yields AUROC improvements of 0.3–0.7 percentage points across scoring functions on Places, SUN, and Textures.",
          "label": "example of the assertion [2]"
        },
        {
          "text": "The improvements hold across backbones (ResNet variants, WideResNet, VGG, MobileNet) and remain when combining AoP with Outlier Exposure (OE).",
          "label": "assertion [3]"
        }
      ]
    },
    "labeling_status": "ok"
  },
  {
    "id": "paper:867752822639165809::conclusion_6",
    "global_id": "gcn_30477c4c38ae4f3f",
    "order": 6,
    "title": "Pruning reduces out-of-distribution overfitting and produces feature separation; non-monotone sparsity dependence",
    "content": "Iterative magnitude pruning (Lottery Ticket Hypothesis (LTH) style with weight rewinding) reduces the late-stage overfitting of out-of-distribution (OOD) detection: as global sparsity is increased from dense toward moderate sparsity, the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) on OOD tasks first increases (pruning removes redundant common features that induce in-distribution (ID) / OOD overlap) and then eventually decreases if pruning becomes too aggressive and removes discriminative capacity. Visualization of final-layer features shows pruned models produce larger ID-versus-OOD feature differences than dense models. Different pruning schedules and methods matter: LTH with weight rewind outperforms random pruning and naive fine-tuning based pruning in OOD metrics, and among during-training pruning methods evaluated (Gradual Magnitude Pruning (GMP), Rigging the Lottery (RigL), Gradual Network with neuroregeneration (GraNet)), iterative/post-training LTH performed best for OOD tasks in CIFAR-10 experiments with ResNet-18.",
    "labeled_segments": {
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "Iterative magnitude pruning (Lottery Ticket Hypothesis (LTH) style with weight rewinding) reduces the late-stage overfitting of out-of-distribution (OOD) detection: as global sparsity is increased from dense toward moderate sparsity, the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) on OOD tasks first increases ",
          "label": "assertion [1]"
        },
        {
          "text": "(pruning removes redundant common features that induce in-distribution (ID) / OOD overlap) ",
          "label": "elaboration"
        },
        {
          "text": "and then eventually decreases if pruning becomes too aggressive and removes discriminative capacity. ",
          "label": "assertion [1]"
        },
        {
          "text": "Visualization of final-layer features shows pruned models produce larger ID-versus-OOD feature differences than dense models. ",
          "label": "proof of the assertion [1]"
        },
        {
          "text": "Different pruning schedules and methods matter: ",
          "label": "assertion [2]"
        },
        {
          "text": "LTH with weight rewind outperforms random pruning and naive fine-tuning based pruning in OOD metrics, and ",
          "label": "assertion [3]"
        },
        {
          "text": "among during-training pruning methods evaluated (Gradual Magnitude Pruning (GMP), Rigging the Lottery (RigL), Gradual Network with neuroregeneration (GraNet)), iterative/post-training LTH performed best for OOD tasks in CIFAR-10 experiments with ResNet-18.",
          "label": "assertion [4]"
        }
      ]
    },
    "labeling_status": "ok"
  },
  {
    "id": "paper:867752822639165809::conclusion_7",
    "global_id": "gcn_350802c4180246cd",
    "order": 7,
    "title": "Ablation: both model averaging and pruning are necessary to realize full Average of Pruning gains",
    "content": "Ablation experiments establish that both components—model averaging (MA) and pruning—are required: (i) pruning alone improves the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) relative to the dense baseline, and (ii) combining pruning with MA (Average of Pruning, AoP) yields the best performance. For example on CIFAR-10 with ResNet-18: baseline AUROC = 91.63%; with pruning alone AUROC increases to 92.94%; with pruning plus MA (AoP) AUROC further increases to 93.32%. Similar trends hold for WideResNet-28-10 and other metrics (area under the precision-recall curve (AUPR), false positive rate at 95% true positive rate (FPR95)), showing complementary roles: pruning reduces overfitting and MA stabilizes the weights/landscape for more reliable scoring.",
    "labeled_segments": {
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "Ablation experiments establish that both components—model averaging (MA) and pruning—are required: ",
          "label": "assertion [1]"
        },
        {
          "text": "(i) pruning alone improves the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) relative to the dense baseline, ",
          "label": "assertion [2]"
        },
        {
          "text": "and (ii) combining pruning with MA (Average of Pruning, AoP) yields the best performance. ",
          "label": "assertion [3]"
        },
        {
          "text": "For example on CIFAR-10 with ResNet-18: baseline AUROC = 91.63%; with pruning alone AUROC increases to 92.94%; with pruning plus MA (AoP) AUROC further increases to 93.32%. ",
          "label": "example of the assertions [2,3]"
        },
        {
          "text": "Similar trends hold for WideResNet-28-10 and other metrics (area under the precision-recall curve (AUPR), false positive rate at 95% true positive rate (FPR95)), ",
          "label": "example of the assertions [2,3]"
        },
        {
          "text": "showing complementary roles: pruning reduces overfitting and MA stabilizes the weights/landscape for more reliable scoring.",
          "label": "assertion [4]"
        }
      ]
    },
    "labeling_status": "ok"
  },
  {
    "id": "paper:867752822639165809::conclusion_8",
    "global_id": "gcn_4369dce469034295",
    "order": 8,
    "title": "Average of Pruning improves misclassification (error) detection as well as out-of-distribution detection",
    "content": "Average of Pruning (AoP) not only improves out-of-distribution (OOD) detection but also enhances misclassification detection (detecting when the classifier will be wrong on in-distribution inputs). On WideResNet-40-2 trained on CIFAR-10, applying AoP to Maximum Softmax Probability (MSP) improves the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) for misclassification detection from 92.51% to 93.23%, reduces the area under the risk-coverage curve (AURC) from \\(7.55\\times 10^{-3}\\) to \\(6.41\\times 10^{-3}\\), and reduces the false positive rate at 95% true positive rate (FPR95) from 40.87% to 38.47%. Similar improvements are observed when AoP is combined with other confidence estimators (e.g., Confidence-aware Learning (CRL)).",
    "labeled_segments": {
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "Average of Pruning (AoP) not only improves out-of-distribution (OOD) detection but also enhances misclassification detection ",
          "label": "assertion [1]"
        },
        {
          "text": "(detecting when the classifier will be wrong on in-distribution inputs).",
          "label": "elaboration"
